E1: portfolio, CASC-J13 FOF (400 scrambled competition problems)

StarExec job 7425, solver 5187 (LisaST-20260908-1107), space 28895, queue public.q
  w=180 cpu=1500 mem=160  -> 128745 MB granted per pair (the queue ceiling, and CASC's own per-solver limit)
Node: Intel Xeon E5-2620 v4 @ 2.10GHz, 8 cores per pair (runsolver --cores 0-7), java 21.0.11
Per pair: 8 strategies started together, one pinned per core, ~11997 MB heap each, none killed early.
Wall-clock budget 180 s, of which 166 s is the search; the rest covers the harness's hard cap, the
interrupt grace, and the parent's collection of the eight rows. Parsing counts against the budget.
Full axiom set (no SLIM), so every problem is attempted.

800 pairs, all complete. 6304 of 6400 worker rows (98.5%). 0 bad proofs, 0 uses_sorry.
e1a 196/400 solved, e1b 192/400: certification costs 4 problems.
BIO006+1 remains unparseable (StackOverflowError in the TPTP front end; see W14).
Repository: ece294f3 on cpp_lisast

Superseded, kept only for comparison:
  e1-casc-j13-fof-run1-parsebias.csv (job 7423) -- the search budget started after parsing, so JVM
    start-up and reading the problem fell outside it; 202 of 400 problems had a worker killed with
    nothing to report. e1a 191, e1b 170.
  e1-casc-j13-fof-run2-nogoal.csv (job 7424) -- the certified path passed no goal clauses to the
    prover, so goal-directed selection was inert and e1b searched differently from e1a; and SLIM=1
    left 8 problems unparseable. e1a 195, e1b 168, which overstated the cost of certification
    almost sevenfold.
